  "notes": "Absence is the parameter. The producers describe the record's power as coming from simplicity rather than layering, so this model is authored as subtraction from the trap parent: the sparsest kick in this batch (1–3 hits a bar, `syncopation` 0.2), non-continuous 8th hats, the fewest melody notes, and one enormous distorted 808 that is the whole low end. The snare roll is the main event marker per the research, which is why it carries the widest velocity ramp here and the drop-out probability is the highest. ⚠ `hihat.continuous` is false because the research says the hats are sparse 8ths and the drums drop out entirely for long stretches; `fillDensity` is held at 0.3 so every seed still gets hats — a model that declares a hat block owes each seed one. ⚠ `timbreHint` is deliberately NOT authored: the research names a bell figure and the trap parent's `dark_bell` already is that, so inheriting is the honest reading. ⚠ `chords.impliedByRiff` is set — the research says a single low drone plus a 3-note bell figure, i.e. one chord is the part; the variety a chord generator owes comes from `chordDurationBeats` and three harmonic rhythms rather than from progressions it does not play. ⚠ The i–VI family is INFERRED, not researched: the research names only static harmony. It carries the low weight and is the first thing to delete if this ever sounds busier than the record. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates this high-medium. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
